  "notes": "The bridge out of 1993 synth funk into the 1999–2001 Aftermath idiom, and the two parameters that say so are the tightest quantize in this batch (0.94) against a genre that sits at 0.84, and a bass that is `mirror_kick` at `followRootsProb` 0.90 — the research calls the shift to \"simple root-motion lines locked to the kick\" a documented move away from the melodic-bass habit of 1993, and locking to the kick is exactly what this model does. Several 2000-era cuts read half-time, so the kick drops to 1 and 3 under a 2&4 snare (`secondaryAnchor` \"3\", `densityPerBar` [2,3]) while the snare gets the biggest layered crack here. Hats are sparse and stop (`continuous` false at 0.30) because the research says so twice: \"sparse hats\", \"louder and drier than mid-90s g-funk\". Harmony is rolling piano and strings in close position, so `voicing.style` is `close` and its register is the lowest here. ⚠ `medium` on the research's high-medium. INFERRED: the progression families — the research names \"2-bar minor loops\" and a producer roster (Battlecat, Quik, Rick Rock, Sir Jinx, Soopafly, Mel-Man, Nottz, Rockwilder, Erick Sermon) but no actual changes, so i–VI and i–VII–VI are the LA-minor default rather than a sourced claim.",
